  "content_description": "Resources Action Item",
  "content_text_body": "The goal of clearly documented procedures is to help new employees or those taking on new roles reach a level of competence by providing a consistent, focused learning experience. Reference documents allow employees working on a project to gain just-in-time knowledge that will enable them to execute at each procedural step.\n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n ### When to create it\n\n \n\n Try to produce support documentation for each new process, product or initiative within your team.\n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n Document as you work. It's faster that way in the end, and you're more likely to complete the documentation on-the-spot than you are after the fact.\n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n ### Who creates it\n\n \n\n The person responsible for the new process/product/initiative is the best person to create the support documentation because she/he knows the task the best. If you have the resources, having one person shadow and take notes while another person goes through each step is a very efficient way to document processes.",
